#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" :question/math-academy-id "e-597" :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty$.\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}x & = 2 ( - 1 ) + 3 = 1 , \\\\ y & = ( - 1 {)}^{3} - 4 ( - 1 ) = - 1 + 4 = 3.\\end{array}$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3)$. The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/f8cfd5f61b0ad19f503ddb05927c280e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTo compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}m = {\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} |}_{t = - 1} & = \\frac{3 ( - 1 {)}^{2} - 4}{2} = - \\frac{1}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)\n$$\n\nand the slope\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" :question/math-academy-id "e-598" :question/problem "The following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n$x=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/b6d3cface8e385a6ebaa2fe18a25c564.png)\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ninto the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{4}) + \\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} + 1, \\\\\ny &= \\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{4}) - 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2\\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right)\n$$\n\nFor this, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwhich means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} & = \\frac{{y}^{\\prime } ( t )}{{x}^{\\prime } ( t )} \\\\  & = \\frac{- \\sin   t - 4 \\cos   2 t}{- 2 \\sin   t + 2 \\cos   2 t} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\sin   t + 4 \\cos   2 t}{2 ( \\cos   2 t - \\sin   t )} .\\end{array}$$\n\nAt the point where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nthe slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}m & = {\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} |}_{t = \\pi  / 4} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\sin   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) + 4 \\cos   ( \\frac{\\pi }{2} )}{2 ( \\cos   ( \\frac{\\pi }{2} ) - \\sin   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) )} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{2} + 4 ( 0 )}{2 ( 0 - \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{2} )} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2) &= -2(x - (\\sqrt{2} + 1)) \\\\\ny - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 2 &= -2x + 2\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\\\\ny + 2x &= \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "72076ddb-4a3a-530f-854c-7b1400cd9859" :question/math-academy-id "e-6240" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty < t < \\infty$.\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while\n\n$$\ny'(t) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ny'(0) = -1\\neq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that when $t = 0$, the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6252" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while\n\n$$\ny'(t) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx^{\\prime }(t) = 6t^{2}, y^{\\prime }(t) = 2t - 1\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{\\prime }(0) &= 2 \\cdot 0 - 1 =-1 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f6ae3b2-630d-5ba6-b03f-f2bd215dc2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084772} {:answer/id #uuid "e80b527e-cb3c-5a74-b5c0-0aeac4525afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084773} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7a0050-2e1f-5220-a412-a2c3dad870a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084774} {:answer/id #uuid "572b1132-3c3f-52cd-b7e2-87c093f3ba29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084775} {:answer/id #uuid "7473bed6-7d3f-5fb2-a6ae-05b81ff46510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f7a0050-2e1f-5220-a412-a2c3dad870a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084774} :answer-field/id #uuid "190540d5-34c4-5fc1-bc2e-ddb4e0d89573" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084771}] :question/id #uuid "cbab7bcf-6a8b-5a33-ae85-61d0acf13fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15570" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\ln (\\sin t), y = \\frac{1}{\\sin t}, t \\in  (0, \\pi )$.\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t = \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0fa5cb-58b2-5991-aeb3-3c72630b91f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085058} {:answer/id #uuid "d40ce140-4fa5-5e19-a6d2-c2ed4122946e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085059} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdb6ae8-5736-5ecb-a2b0-06e71866c8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{3π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085060} {:answer/id #uuid "1f118b88-363d-549d-8d78-43f7f7a3fe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085061} {:answer/id #uuid "416acf6e-914c-5f39-8409-93bf2a0d33b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d40ce140-4fa5-5e19-a6d2-c2ed4122946e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085059} :answer-field/id #uuid "b486e99f-74f6-58c2-873a-74bcf30e0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085057}] :question/id #uuid "fbd878d8-e318-5109-beb9-89ecefca7f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-21081" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\sin t + 7, y = 4\\cos t + 10, 0 \\le t < 2\\pi $.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a418921-4ddc-515d-be59-063b11f1b91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085070} {:answer/id #uuid "c626ea12-f712-5a6d-abb3-d3c9454f17e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085071} {:answer/id #uuid "dbef1b02-004e-5d07-ac10-f99bb4bdf640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085072} {:answer/id #uuid "7265d5ee-6cfb-5775-a948-08318ea9de4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085073} {:answer/id #uuid "7b461d49-e04e-5a80-b9a1-1a16086bbb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7265d5ee-6cfb-5775-a948-08318ea9de4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085073} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8768f88-2f81-52cb-99c3-baa01a649588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085069}] :question/id #uuid "ad8de0d6-0fb0-5332-a3cd-f5bf44efff31" :question/math-academy-id "q-21207" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = e^{2t}, y = t^{2}, 0 \\le t \\le 2$.\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc91621-aeb5-5b9b-8967-1863095c9fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = (x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086216} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f0b63-39aa-55ed-bf75-6b620acc0edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = 2(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086217} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e80624-b2f0-5ef1-92d0-df5481905b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{24}{11}x + \\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086218} {:answer/id #uuid "0613faa1-bfa6-50e0-8304-289efb1c481e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = 2(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086219} {:answer/id #uuid "31b8a3af-c940-5339-b1e9-dd6b2e0c4f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = (x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0613faa1-bfa6-50e0-8304-289efb1c481e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 2 = 2(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086219} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f78337f-4f92-5159-96b2-5bbb8f36eda9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086215}] :question/id #uuid "b94e72be-f23a-535e-b719-cf664553611b" :question/math-academy-id "q-6857" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/q-6857.png)\n\nA curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 2t^{3} - 3t, y = 3t^{2} - 5, - \\infty  < t < \\infty $.\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t = 1$." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
